WEBVTT


1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:07.490
Hello everyone, welcome to the 9th topic of this module.

2
00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:18.210
Today I'll be talking to you about a slightly more applied extension of what

3
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:20.830
we were talking about in the last 8 weeks.

4
00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:29.740
So just to remind you, in the first 5 weeks we covered the foundations of Isabelle,

5
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:37.600
and in the last 3 weeks we worked on the correctness of some simple function programs like merge sort,

6
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.880
and then we talked about data structures, and then we talked about abstract data types.

7
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:48.320
In this week and the next one we will talk about more complicated algorithms, how to model them in Isabelle and how to formally verify

8
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.620
them, employing all the techniques that we have seen in the last 8 weeks.

9
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:04.940
So today I'll be talking about how to model graph algorithms in general, and more specifically depth-first search.

10
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:12.900
So I'll be talking about 3 things. First I'll give you an introduction to how to formalize directed graphs and depth-first search.

11
00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:19.160
So what are directed graphs and what is depth-first search, and then how to formalize directed graphs in Isabelle.

12
00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:26.740
Second I'll be talking about how to have abstract data types, an abstract data type for a graph,

13
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.040
and third I'll be talking about how to model the entirety of depth-first search in Isabelle,

14
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.030
and how to make it executable.

15
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:41.310
So let's talk about directed graphs.

16
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:48.430
What is a directed graph? A directed graph is something like this network that we can see here.

17
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:56.260
So in this network you have vertices A,

18
00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:05.240
B, C, D and E, and you have edges between say A and B,

19
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.260
so this is a directed edge, and so on.

20
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:18.700
So you must have seen that already in a number of areas before today, but my focus today will be about how to model these

21
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.420
directed graphs in Isabelle.

22
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:31.090
So as I mentioned in the abstract data types lecture, whenever we're modeling something in Isabelle for the purpose of building algorithms,

23
00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.810
we might need to have 2 types of models.

24
00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:41.930
The first is an abstract mathematical model, and this is what we'll be talking about now for directed graphs.

25
00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:53.540
And the second one is something more geared towards building algorithms on it and making it executable.

26
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.180
So I'll first start with the mathematical model of directed graphs.

27
00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:07.800
So one way to model directed graphs in Isabelle is using mathematical sets in Isabelle.

28
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:15.700
So if you remember we had this type set that takes a type variable as an argument.

29
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:25.330
So for instance we had the alpha set, or an alpha set is an Isabelle mathematical set for instance.

30
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:33.440
In the case of directed graphs, we're not going to be doing an alpha set, but we're going to do a set that takes a pair as an argument,

31
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:39.720
where every pair in the set represents an edge of the graph.

32
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.740
So for instance, if we want to talk about this directed graph, represented as a mathematical set in Isabelle,

33
00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.180
it would look like this.

34
00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:54.780
So you would have a pair for the first edge, AB, a pair for the second edge, BC,

35
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.780
and BD, and so on.

36
00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:10.890
So this is a mathematical representation of directed graphs that I will be using in Isabelle in the rest of this module.

37
00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:21.320
And it's quite nice in terms of allowing you to prove theorems formally about graphs,

38
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:28.560
because mathematical sets are easy to deal with in Isabelle, because there is a lot of automation and simplification rules for them and so on.

39
00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:35.730
So after we define graphs,

40
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.950
or the type of the graphs as shown here, as a set of pairs,

41
00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:46.390
the next thing we do is define concepts on top of that graph.

42
00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.550
For example, what is the neighborhood of a vertex, what is the set of vertices in a graph, and so forth.

43
00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:59.680
So for instance, to define the neighborhood of a vertex, we define a function that takes a function neighborhood,

44
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:09.180
it takes a graph, a set of pairs, and takes a vertex, and gives you a set of vertices which are the neighborhood of that vertex.

45
00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:15.580
And in order for us to formalize that in Isabelle, we use so-called set comprehensions.

46
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:26.320
So here in this format, this thing is a set comprehension, where we're saying we want all vertices,

47
00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:38.160
given a vertex U in a graph G, we want all the vertices V such that there is an edge going from U to V in G.

48
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:47.420
So this is the set of all the neighbors of U, which is the set of all vertices on which an edge outgoing

49
00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.980
from U is an incident.

50
00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:58.810
So for instance, if we call that graph G,

51
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.940
and then I say, if we just call that graph G,

52
00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:14.200
so let's say the neighborhood of G say A, we would get the set of B and C.

53
00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:25.000
And that's nothing more complicated set comprehension, but it's the same idea, it's the set of vertices of the whole graph.

54
00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:31.280
So this is a function called dVs, it takes a graph, it takes a set of pairs, and it turns to a set of vertices of V set.

55
00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:41.840
And the way it's done is firstly we say, I want all vertices V1 and V2 such that there is an edge V1,

56
00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.000
V2 in the graph,

57
00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:53.360
so an edge from V1 to V2, occurring in the graph, each pair of vertices which occur in the graph as an edge,

58
00:06:53.360 --> 00:07:01.240
I want to turn them into an ordered pair or a set of vertices, a set with two vertices,

59
00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.540
so V1 and V2,

60
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:09.660
and then I take all these sets and take their union, their big union, and that's the set of vertices in the graph.

61
00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:17.180
So to make that concrete with the example, so dVs of our graph would look like,

62
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.400
for this example graph that we have there,

63
00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:30.120
it would look like the set, the big union of the set AB and

64
00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:39.380
the set BC and the set AC and

65
00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:41.860
so on,

66
00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:47.880
you do this for every edge, and then you take the big union of all of that, so you'd get A,

67
00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.500
B, and C and so on.

68
00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:57.510
So as I mentioned, this abstract mathematical representation of graphs is good for proofs,

69
00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:59.510
because the graph is just a set,

70
00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:03.870
and there's a lot of automation in Isabelle and simplification rules and so on to deal with that,

71
00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:11.850
but the disadvantage of this representation, as is the disadvantage of most mathematical representations in Isabelle,

72
00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:18.890
is that it's not executable, because the set may be infinite, or we don't have an order to pick a neighbor, for instance,

73
00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:25.270
so you cannot write an algorithm based on this notion of graphs.

74
00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:32.720
You need an executable version of graphs, you cannot use these sets to build a graph for an executable algorithm.

75
00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:39.940
So this notion of graphs is just for us to specify properties of graphs,

76
00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:44.820
rather than to write algorithms that run on them.

77
00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:55.340
Another notion of graphs, which is very basic,

78
00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.920
is the notion of a walk.

79
00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:07.900
What is a walk in a graph? For instance, informally,

80
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:15.480
a walk is the list of vertices, such that consecutive vertices in this list,

81
00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.630
every two consecutive vertices in this list, are joined by an edge.

82
00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:28.680
So for instance, in this graph here, if I say a, b,

83
00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:33.980
and then c, that is our vwalk,

84
00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:43.340
because, let me put it under definition here, a, b,

85
00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.840
c is a vwalk, because there is an edge from a to b,

86
00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:53.820
and there is an edge from b to c, so every two consecutive vertices have an edge connecting them.

87
00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.410
So there is this edge, and that edge.

88
00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:06.530
While, for example, b, a, c is not a vwalk,

89
00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.170
because there is not an edge going from b to a,

90
00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:11.070
there is only an edge going from a to b.

91
00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.420
So, informally, that's what a vwalk is.

92
00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:22.010
In this example we define it, again, as a function, but this time it's a recursive function.

93
00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:29.180
So here we would say, it's a function that takes a graph, and a list of vertices, which is the walk,

94
00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:35.180
so this is the walk, or the potential walk, and returns to us bool,

95
00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:41.240
so either true or false, indicating whether the second argument is a walk or not.

96
00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:52.880
So, how is that defined? It's defined recursively, in terms of the given list of vertices.

97
00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.200
So for an empty list of vertices, or in other words, an empty walk, is always going to be true,

98
00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.380
because it's a walk that has nothing in it.

99
00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:12.560
A singleton walk, so a list that has only one vertex, will also be considered a walk in only one case,

100
00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.340
if and only if that vertex occurs in the graph.

101
00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:23.160
So, for instance, in our case, we can say the empty list is a walk,

102
00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:29.880
the singleton list that only has a is a walk, the singleton list that only has b is a walk,

103
00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:31.240
so that's just our convention.

104
00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:37.880
We define, we call vertices, singleton vertices that occur in the graph a walk,

105
00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:46.140
but the most interesting case, which is the case when the graph, when the list of vertices has at least two members in it,

106
00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.440
has at least two elements, has two vertices in it,

107
00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:57.660
in this case, the vwalk is true if and only if there is an edge between u and v in the graph,

108
00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:02.060
and the rest of the list of vertices is also a walk.

109
00:12:02.060 --> 00:12:10.340
So, in the recursive case, if there is an edge from u to v, and the rest is a walk,

110
00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:16.620
the rest of the list of vertices is a walk, then the whole thing is a walk.

111
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:22.040
So that's the recursive definition for a vwalk in Isabelle.

112
00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:27.680
Then we define another topic, another notion called vwalk_bet,

113
00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:34.470
and here we take a graph, a first vertex, a list of vertices,

114
00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:35.870
and the last vertex,

115
00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:51.020
and then we say, that definition is basically saying that p, vwalk_bet

116
00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:56.120
u and v,

117
00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:01.620
and starting at u and ending at v, so that definition is defined such that you have vwalk,

118
00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:07.940
so that the list of vertices p has to be a walk, has to be a walk in the graph,

119
00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:14.700
and it should not be an empty one, and the first vertex in it,

120
00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:17.230
the head of that list of vertices,

121
00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.610
should be u, and the last vertex should be v.

122
00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:25.020
This is another notion that we define for graphs.

123
00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:32.640
So this concludes the mathematical definition of graphs in Isabelle,

124
00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:45.350
which is not what you would run

125
00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:47.640
with an algorithm,

126
00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:55.560
but it is the mathematical notion for graphs in which we define most of our interesting properties of graphs,

127
00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.560
like for example, what is a tree, what is a connected component, and so on.

